swer-field/id #uuid "486d748e-e1d6-538d-bcd1-fc10f7aff262" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb3a08e1-046c-5655-8701-2c4bac14eb96" :question/math-academy-id "q-80234" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-5$ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $-5$ is negative, so it does *not* belong to $N,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\notin N.\n$$\n\n- However, $-5$ is an integer, so it does belong to $Z,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Every integer is a rational, real, and complex number. Consequently, $-5$ belongs to $Q,R,$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\in Q,\\,-5\\in R,\\,-5\\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Z,Q,R,$ and $C.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3b6502-2609-5694-8337-343cf338b3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345856} {:answer/id #uuid "74a024f1-1785-588f-a3c0-b3929f2b8574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346380} {:answer/id #uuid "a9089c55-dfe7-5ac8-86fd-78d0e5790c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346821} {:answer/id #uuid "beeb9025-e29a-5c05-affb-c22296617bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347003} {:answer/id #uuid "cff3b3b6-cdf0-59d1-ba63-da91f007f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff3b3b6-cdf0-59d1-ba63-da91f007f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347170} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6c5495-43b4-5556-a6fd-3b0bbef42494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2a941ac-46f5-56a9-b5ba-03889446453a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80336" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-\\pi $\n- $\\sqrt{3}$\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$\n- $0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-\\pi $ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{3}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cecec7a2-9199-5699-be63-afdf78624abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056479} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ec761-f38f-563b-b8d9-c38f8c04b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056480} {:answer/id #uuid "09585361-cdae-5b7c-9567-af2048f0375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056481} {:answer/id #uuid "4716d9a1-77e4-5d03-8256-b7691d68238d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056482} {:answer/id #uuid "69dceb08-43f5-565b-bd2a-0344b15ee7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3834be7c-3d92-5a42-ba11-b5873d0a2001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0e16856-fe81-51dc-ba71-d4bb0b707df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364956} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2581fc-f5d1-56c8-ab61-5d417f0d30e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364957} {:answer/id #uuid "14d9082f-208d-5d34-9c63-524f45f1904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364958} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c099fe-52bc-5e28-83a9-4aad7f968450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364959} {:answer/id #uuid "df4cf5da-869a-50b7-a8cd-35b980cbe9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9c099fe-52bc-5e28-83a9-4aad7f968450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43375d0-11ca-5e43-8be7-2100aee7f584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d9bc49-c94e-5174-9068-2abf75c42684" :question/math-academy-id "q-128490" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\n- $det(M)=10$\n- $det(M)=-5$\n- $M$ is a singular matrix\n\n \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871a7617-5883-54c8-9745-ccda8b526b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057037} {:answer/id #uuid "c38869d4-7e70-54e4-aa9a-df7864f2559a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057038} {:answer/id #uuid "da12571e-3a0a-5c9e-82f3-a71442fefc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057039} {:answer/id #uuid "06b20c6d-4149-51cd-89bd-f13d224528b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057040} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6d0636-7799-50e9-8446-ae1ecf71fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057041}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5ba7e8-f49b-5ea6-8c98-ac67aae3ec8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b811f16-43eb-5f85-84c8-55929421d66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378983} {:answer/id #uuid "78a24d3f-be42-5f68-a888-0034cc2ef78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378984} {:answer/id #uuid "0118e984-414e-5948-aa4c-f8da0babefc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378985} {:answer/id #uuid "69d0ce7a-a4de-5d93-b271-a087f1d61b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378986} {:answer/id #uuid "547d50fe-8135-5c3f-9493-d83c061ec333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547d50fe-8135-5c3f-9493-d83c061ec333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378987} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0553c1-dc69-5b86-83b2-1778e74ee087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37ad276a-88ce-596c-9a24-406a32aa6e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-171334" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** $10\\ge 1,$ **then** $3\\in R.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If $10<1,$ then $3\\notin R.$\n- If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$\n- If $3\\in R,$ then $10\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef745713-2d35-5b7c-8ffa-849019bc9a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379162} {:answer/id #uuid "09ee9ee7-3301-505d-b0b4-fa2550135d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379163} {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e643c-4c88-5ee5-a9cb-bc94557a292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379165} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f93cbd-83f5-5a7a-b05a-db016463f018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f9c689-8577-52b8-aa74-632d353fd931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "866aa1bc-982d-58ab-8e07-dd5148fa5235" :question/math-academy-id "q-182470" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e280238-ad8a-5e05-8b0b-bf5189786b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288225} {:answer/id #uuid "30cbcedf-ffb7-53a8-b4e5-7be6041abfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288354} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5f6490-ca71-5ec7-be79-a8915ee68af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288569} {:answer/id #uuid "bb99c0e2-c740-5862-af41-80fb14c428a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289269} {:answer/id #uuid "dd115f3a-f93f-5d14-9654-f7cad87fdfb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb99c0e2-c740-5862-af41-80fb14c428a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289269} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b49d71-8078-55fb-9d6f-ec2865ffbbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7636651-ac27-54dd-bac3-a58cbd0f8a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-23191" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\n- $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n- $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$\n- $-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1912b5b-58a0-5491-a50c-b728a4ed3e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059299} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f7c0b0-c3a5-549e-b078-15d79f0248eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059300} {:answer/id #uuid "06c85cfe-b123-504b-9faf-324c33d747ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059301} {:answer/id #uuid "0e43444a-08a5-579c-96df-f1622d1ebfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ and $-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059302} {:answer/id #uuid "38b74efe-eb9e-5251-9500-fed3d79db7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059303}] :answer-field/id #uuid "252ad927-f137-587e-abae-e173b3313976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059298}] :question/id #uuid "f04c86de-5ed5-52ee-bc8b-1a0c07c06519" :question/math-academy-id "q-9657" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ x & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & x + 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis $8$, find all the possible values of $x$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f09ed10-b680-5e9c-99e7-04c22b6a5202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361046} {:answer/id #uuid "4a66f65a-11a7-5f42-83a1-b14b35b1c089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361158} {:answer/id #uuid "51594ef1-0fed-5155-b685-7f55eca2a956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361194} {:answer/id #uuid "86cefcfa-29d4-56e3-b026-a13b84142878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361439} {:answer/id #uuid "be424c64-a1cc-5c27-9b4e-5599f2b8e337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a66f65a-11a7-5f42-83a1-b14b35b1c089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c65d0d4-2f35-53f8-943b-a9a971d3e887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4279505-241a-54e3-b9b3-ff9b46d09cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-9838" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\n- $det(F)=0$\n- $F$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(F)=-2$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417dddb9-3d7e-5321-bc3b-a56d08fb8f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269861} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4fb9f-5291-5506-a132-c61ed515c509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270030} {:answer/id #uuid "863818eb-76ee-5680-aefe-471f0560a700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270432} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89f69d-f935-5d5a-9945-e731ff500fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270809} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a2da3b-00af-5c91-81ba-0422e4ecaae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "863818eb-76ee-5680-aefe-471f0560a700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270432} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9121d68-5ccf-5812-9b48-da6c2dc5d3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "362ddce8-046e-5791-a78c-7440cb5f1723" :question/math-academy-id "q-141508" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f49ed44236dcfabd3276187899bd2d7d300cbc1c280c228cda0bc2e3599fe7a8.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph III curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1945cb36-7580-5d48-a7f4-e9cae6ecc884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272607} {:answer/id #uuid "6775f336-d17a-5722-a3f3-1ab01f08e18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273277} {:answer/id #uuid "7717dbb6-0383-51e1-8d37-d4fcfd7f6979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273401} {:answer/id #uuid "c40a2e67-3400-59c5-b962-49f77794a29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274089} {:answer/id #uuid "e737be8a-4f9d-5999-a2c2-fee95b9bd888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7717dbb6-0383-51e1-8d37-d4fcfd7f6979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273401} :answer-field/id #uuid "12379981-02c5-5819-af48-54f9acb54a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d96aa7c-3228-53f8-9efa-38bbf767192d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152832" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x<1 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425a8ccf-2901-5fad-8994-d86ff4862178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288482} {:answer/id #uuid "46edec4b-e114-52e7-9b3a-545cf6af4183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288520} {:answer/id #uuid "57dc3dea-4691-599d-a7b0-24348c173e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288641} {:answer/id #uuid "65588d49-6e49-5a26-beca-7b1eb1782e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288747} {:answer/id #uuid "a19844a1-e882-51f3-a425-d313d09a4cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46edec4b-e114-52e7-9b3a-545cf6af4183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288520} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9af23a-abaf-5a94-80da-099d1a4a1165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fa08ecc-691c-5923-94fb-19ce5c958e14" :question/math-academy-id "q-21502" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cfc260-e8fe-57d4-ad54-4b868b37ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288660} {:answer/id #uuid "66d6f3bf-0042-5273-b186-aed0e736c5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288753} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b7b58a-5f27-5686-b835-eeb3986ac127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289584} {:answer/id #uuid "eab07fee-4975-5076-9f67-782c1e81e08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289586} {:answer/id #uuid "eca5348d-66fa-5683-af8f-c0e85df5fe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59cfc260-e8fe-57d4-ad54-4b868b37ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288660} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce046a0d-27c7-5c04-a831-89f387c8a392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13f8cc2a-4183-5051-a10e-eea4f7de407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21528" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e11493-093d-5677-9cce-796074ba130e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061750} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8e750-c89b-5ba7-b458-68ac026f484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061751} {:answer/id #uuid "d85f6949-16e9-59c0-8f4b-cd4eba8557d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061752} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ae605-3c4c-5bbe-bc7a-ba41af30bda2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061753} {:answer/id #uuid "7aec39cd-06b6-51e7-8fac-330d7d637bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061754}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6adffb-ec69-5386-895c-90f005cfeb37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1b3034-251a-59f4-b113-773134be1def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396649} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a7e325-bb6e-50d6-8115-8a687cfd729f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396650} {:answer/id #uuid "b53f607f-c200-5ff6-8ced-e452dc4d4a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396651} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8d5f4e-4f95-5ba3-ba2b-62b542d4c351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396652} {:answer/id #uuid "0481ca8b-da90-5343-99ae-63643fac6ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a7e325-bb6e-50d6-8115-8a687cfd729f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396650} :answer-field/id #uuid "041ff950-f3f8-5661-8897-41d87f43abe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "542cb4a0-6380-5eaf-91e8-44f9300f10c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22428" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\nII. The global minimum value is $e-2$\nIII. The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47eeb60e-719b-5602-84c7-90cb6deec8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288528} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb321c2-7253-5483-ba97-0c74bb61c543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288890} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c0dbb8-20fd-51e4-b050-0b7e781ecab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289234} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6bfbed-5bb5-5686-ac6f-9cc01f01ddd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289417} {:answer/id #uuid "edf9ea6a-4ee5-57cd-b511-a2c16bdf0db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf9ea6a-4ee5-57cd-b511-a2c16bdf0db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289600} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b58445-ac56-549b-a9ce-76b4649b0822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4e43127-8c70-53b8-9c7f-f4ea673dfe39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2459" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and VI are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddbaf22-b2da-5cac-89d6-79657d1917e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327193} {:answer/id #uuid "38166c27-d67a-5474-b8d3-477a1d994bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327422} {:answer/id #uuid "67e71fa5-0f8e-5bf3-ba6a-19044051dc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327832} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f4350a-0532-53d3-b11d-74ddeccd5599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328629} {:answer/id #uuid "e217d73f-503c-5a8b-bea1-62ff0da70229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ddbaf22-b2da-5cac-89d6-79657d1917e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327193} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3c597f-dca3-58b6-a43b-12a74d177733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e7b61b7-5224-5508-b07f-2f649466a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-48352" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\n- $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f1768a-00cf-5990-839f-9b55fea77e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327104} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1c07e1-9fff-5f40-a8ad-7edce6a770be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327590} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce1a30f-a7bc-5276-9c90-cdc6d75360b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327877} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ca6e83-abd9-5c73-9b48-5d3c5c8f9650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328544} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f2ca22-1ace-56cd-b430-511129061c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce1a30f-a7bc-5276-9c90-cdc6d75360b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327877} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb996c9a-67a2-5027-93fd-d9434805a61d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9f8e923-af43-56e4-8d9a-484b2d6f194a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48499" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02802b8a-ad48-5fd1-975c-79af63f75708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326963} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb041e-e81a-5ae2-ad9a-b4f698d36e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327327} {:answer/id #uuid "7d14afd6-5f71-59b6-9093-b32596617729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328012} {:answer/id #uuid "ac82410e-33ed-533e-9802-c4f1995a88f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328430} {:answer/id #uuid "fda5c68f-207a-5ace-967b-c1ac92763cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb041e-e81a-5ae2-ad9a-b4f698d36e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327327} :answer-field/id #uuid "c55405a2-2f5f-5f1c-be55-cda64eee77fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9157fc37-ed6c-586e-91c2-30b330ac595f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48689" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a78286a-924a-5b53-b1ff-20b6e774f358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324064} {:answer/id #uuid "38c11f6c-ec31-5baf-88b1-d11c59ff1a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324432} {:answer/id #uuid "4726d096-c73d-5a7c-a29b-ed3c56e58ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324547} {:answer/id #uuid "563239d5-e805-5360-a191-da957f460fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324689} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aeaf56-06ad-5d25-bdf7-6801ed375e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aeaf56-06ad-5d25-bdf7-6801ed375e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326117} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7f8325-4242-5de7-ae67-9b6277a9588d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f98bb945-e8b3-5349-97c2-0b4b023243ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38547" :question/problem "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "722e0e8e-3fc1-5fbd-88cf-411072a4507a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324918} {:answer/id #uuid "b40f8d96-4ca7-526f-86d8-48a08a6b16e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325518} {:answer/id #uuid "dc523b40-0866-5918-9413-b50c426ed32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325893} {:answer/id #uuid "e375eb61-1500-5859-ab7f-3838c7b51348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325970} {:answer/id #uuid "f2630056-1248-50c4-8e02-0d4c0c2ab21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2630056-1248-50c4-8e02-0d4c0c2ab21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326106} :answer-field/id #uuid "750babed-a888-595e-9f8e-ce04cb646a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff6510c0-b13d-5580-9ffe-72088a4d30a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-38548" :question/problem "![](images/89/89299bca567844fbc51f0bc245acea2b62e6c0de387e18b6052a6608605c0b48.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=f(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} {:answer/id #uuid "29de8dce-e7c7-5fc2-94a7-5179e0c2f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327294} {:answer/id #uuid "440c0fb1-d639-5470-acec-fe52787433f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327530} {:answer/id #uuid "937287c6-1b15-5c81-835f-d6dddc1cd449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328220} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29cc74-1bf5-5fbc-b9ad-88af91611a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} :answer-field/id #uuid "62261799-63f8-547f-a1a7-46cbd5868baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26128fd-a27e-5a35-8575-639ff9311589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067373} {:answer/id #uuid "b3835136-311e-52c3-8f9d-f243c85a4875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f192a9-8305-5e68-97be-10e4dac05d62" 